**Objetivo del trabajo**

El Oficial de Logístico está a cargo de la gestión logística de ACTED a nivel nacional, asume los roles y funciones actuando dentro de los límites de la autoridad delegada y de conformidad con los procedimientos y directrices del departamento de logística, conforme a las políticas y procedimientos logísticos de ACTED.

## Cadena de mando

Bajo la autoridad de:

- Director País / Regional
- Encargo del departamento de Logística
- Responsable directo y exclusivo a los procesos de compras, contratación de bienes y servicios, y gestión de inventario de los proyectos ACTED - REACH en Colombia y Venezuela.

**OBJETIVOS ESPECIFICOS**

- Realizar cotizaciones, estudios de mercado, y levantamiento de expedientes de compra, según lineamientos del supervisor/a directo/a y las políticas de compras de ACTED;
- Coordinar con el equipo logístico de ACTED y proveedores la gestión de servicios de transporte, hospedaje y compra de bienes y servicios;
- Comunicar de manera frecuente, clara y proactiva avances y/o dificultades con procesos logísticos, y de contratación de bienes y servicios;
- Aportar a los reportes logísticos conforme a instrucciones del área logística ACTED.
- responsable de la correcta gestión del inventario de insumos y bienes.
- Mantenerse actualizado en torno a las actividades y procesos logísticos de la organización;
- Garantizar que los procesos logísticos en los que participe cumplen con los estándares y requerimientos logísticos institucionales;
- Realizar cualquier otra actividad relacionada con el objetivo del contrato relacionada con el departamento logístico, administrativo y de gestión documental, así como todas aquellas demás funciones que por las características inherentes a su cargo y condiciones le sean asignadas.
- **DEBERES Y RESPONSABILIDADES**
- Compras
- Realizar y controlar todos los procesos de compras: Order form Cotizaciones, PM, PO, Memos, contrato para revisión/ firma del supervisor/a directa;
- Asegurarse de seguir los procedimientos de compras según el escenario respectivo y las políticas de ACTED;
- Actuar como punto focal con proveedores de bienes y servicios contratados por ACTED.
- Garantizar que la documentación sobre adquisiciones se realice y archive de acuerdo con los procedimientos de ACTED;
- Hacer seguimiento a los procesos de validación, firmas y aprobación de compras, asegurando que las compras y registros pasan entre los equipos logísticos, de finanzas y nivel de coordinación.
- Gestión del inventario
- Responsable de la gestión y mantenimiento de los inventarios;
- Responsable de la gestión a los procedimientos de recepción y envío de bienes;
- Usar y mantener el formato de inventario y realizar un inventario físico mensual;
- Asegurarse que los equipos usan correctamente los formatos de inventario.
- Gestión de los Activos
- Elaboración y control de la lista de activos;
- Elaboración y seguimiento de las comunicaciones;
- Gestionar procesos de ingreso y salida de activos;
- Comprobar que cada uno de los activos está debidamente registrado y archivado;
- Asegurarse de tener activos disponibles y en buen estado para cada misión de la organización.
- Reportes
- Reportar novedades de manera pronta, clara y proactiva al responsable del departamento de Logística. .
- Participar semanalmente en la reunión de coordinación logística de ACTED.
- Preparar reportes sobre avances en procesos logísticos, conforme a la información y tiempos indicados por el supervisor/a directo.
- Transportes
- Apoyar el proceso de planeación logística de desplazamientos en terreno, por medio de revisión de medios de transporte y proveedores de servicios disponibles;
- gestionar la reserva y compra de vuelos, servicios de transporte terrestre y fluvial para proyectos de las misiones.
- Responsable de la gestión y seguimiento de los vehículos y desplazamientos en misiones de terreno
- Comunicación interna
- Generar una dinámica de trabajo armónica, respetuosa y eficiente con el equipo de FLATS de ACTED para lograr la mejor ejecución de los procesos logísticos;
- Monitorear de manera constante los avances en los procesos logísticos, y generar alertas cuando se prevean demoras o dificultades que puedan llegar a afectar la ejecución de los proyectos;
- Proponer de manera ágil y proactiva, respuestas y soluciones a barreras en los procesos logísticos y en el cumplimiento a requerimientos de compras y contratación de servicios.
- Habilidades interpersonales:
- Amabilidad, aceptación y solidaridad • Empatía • Escucha activa • Respeto,
- ### Competencias:
- Dinamismo • Lógica - Trabajo en equipo • Responsabilidad • Compromiso
- Orientado a los resultados • Transparencia – alta atención al detalle - Alta capacidad de adaptación

## PERFIL PROFESIONAL/REQUISITOS

- Educación universitaria en carreras como: administración logística, administración de empresas, o carreras afines.
- Mínimo un (1) año de experiencia en procesos logísticos, cotizaciones, compras, contratación de proveedores de bienes y servicios, y manejo de inventarios;
- Experiencia en gestión logística con organizaciones del sector social y humanitario será vista favorablemente;
- Deseable conocimiento de proveedores logísticos, de transporte y hospedaje en diversas regiones del país.
- Conocimientos y experiencia con sistemas operativos Microsoft Windows

## CONDICIONES CONTRACTUALES:

## • Contrato de prestación de servicios bajo la figura de Honorarios Profesionales durante 3 meses con posibilidad de renovación según disponibilidad presupuestaria y desempeño.

- **Lugar de trabajo: Oficina de Acted en Caracas.**
- Honorarios profesionales según la escala de la organización.
- Prestación de Servicios de forma presencia y traslados frecuentes a zonas afectadas por el doblete
- sísmico.
- **Caracas – Distrito Capital** Trabajo 100% presencial y viajes a regiones.
- Todo candidato/a debe contar con permiso válido para trabajar en Venezuela y su renovación no dependerá del vínculo con la organización.

## Acerca de COOPI

COOPI Cooperazione Internazionale es una ONG laica e independiente, fundada en Milán en 1965. Lleva a cabo proyectos de desarrollo para contribuir a la lucha contra las desigualdades y programas de emergencia para apoyar a las personas afectadas por desastres naturales o conflictos. Actualmente, COOPI está presente en 33 países de África, Oriente Medio, América Latina y Caribe.

Desde 2019 COOPI establece una presencia en Venezuela, y a partir de julio de 2020, inicia un programa regional que se implementa en asociación con organizaciones venezolanas y en coordinación con instituciones locales e internacionales, en el marco de los planes de respuesta vigentes. En los últimos años COOPI ha ampliado sus intervenciones y actualmente ejecuta proyectos con diferentes donantes en Caracas, Sucre, Delta Amacuro y Bolivar, en protección (GBV, trata y tráfico de personas), salud sexual reproductiva, WASH (agua, saneamiento e higiene), alimentación escolar y fortalecimiento istitucional.

**Objetivo/descripción del puesto**

El Consultor de Seguridad será responsable de la gestión y actualización de todos los aspectos relacionados con la seguridad de las misiones de COOPI en Venezuela, apoyando en el análisis de contexto, la actualización de planes de seguridad, la capacitación del personal y en la gestion de incidentes, con el fin de garantizar un entorno seguro para las operaciones humanitarias.

La posición tiene un carácter de asesoría técnica a tiempo parcial, orientada a asegurar el seguimiento continuo de la situación de seguridad, acompañar a la Coordinación País y a los equipos en la toma de decisiones operativas, y mantener actualizados los sistemas, herramientas y documentos de seguridad ya desarrollados por la misión, combinando trabajo de análisis con presencia regular y apoyo práctico según las necesidades del contexto. Se requiere persona con nacionalidad Venezolana o permiso de residencia en el país.

**Áreas de responsabilidad**

**Análisis y seguimiento del contexto**

- Elaborar reportes periodicos de monitoreo de contexto y análisis de riesgos, con énfasis en dinámicas políticas, medidas de seguridad pública, y su impacto en las operaciones humanitarias.
- Mantener comunicación directa y regular con los foros de seguridad humanitaria (UNDSS, OCHA, INSO, ONG Forum) y sintetizar alertas relevantes para COOPI.

**Revisión y actualización de documentos de seguridad**

- Mantener actualizados y adaptar los documentos de seguridad de la misión a la evolución del contexto elaborados en la fase anterior incluyendo;
- Analisis de riesgos y de vulnerabilidades de COOPI en las diferentes zonas de intervención y medidas de reducción de riesgos;
- Planes de seguridad del país (misión y bases);
- Planes de contingencia de las diferentes bases: hibernación, reubicación, evacuación y Medevac (evacuación médica);
- Procedimientos Operativos Estándar (SOP) para la gestión de distintos aspectos vinculados a seguridad: movimientos y transporte, interacción con autoridades, el manejo de incidentes de seguridad (asaltos, secuestros, etc.);
- Vademécum para personal expatriado en Venezuela: pre-arrival briefing, reglamento movimientos y seguridad general expatriados;
- Recomendaciones básicas de seguridad digital y manejo seguro de información sensible durante misiones, desplazamientos y comunicación con equipos de terreno, en coordinación con las áreas de Administración/IT y Coordinación País.
- Ser el punto focal para la implementación y revisión continua de los lineamientos de seguridad, deber de cuidado y gestión de riesgos operativos de la misión, en coordinación con la Coordinación País y las áreas pertinentes;
- Realizar reportes periódicos de situación de contexto de seguridad;
- Participar en la readaccion de análisis de seguridad para informes a donantes y propuestas de financiación.

**Capacitación y acompanamiento del Personal:**

- Organizar y ejecutar formaciones periodicas dirigidas al equipo de COOPI, sobre temas clave de seguridad en el contexto humanitario, incluyendo protocolos ante emergencias y análisis de riesgos;
- Realizar briefing de seguridad al personal de coordinación y de las bases;
- Actualizar los temas de formación según el contexto de seguridad de Venezuela y las normativas internacionales;
- Realiar misiones de monitoreo y acompañamiento a las actividades en terreno.

**Gestión de Incidentes de Seguridad:**

- Actuar como el principal punto de contacto en caso de incidentes de seguridad (con autoridades locales, grupos armados u otras amenazas a la seguridad);
- Asesorar a los equipos de base en la preparación de kits de hibernación y en la revisión de rutas seguras;
- Apoyar la revisión y actualización de los procedimientos de planificación, autorización, seguimiento y reporte de movimientos del personal, vehículos e insumos, incluyendo criterios de suspensión o reprogramación de desplazamientos según el nivel de riesgo;
- Coordinar la respuesta ante incidentes, siguiendo los protocolos establecidos, bajo la supervisión del jefe de misión.

**Asesoría General y Representación:**

- Proporcionar asesoría en la toma de decisiones sobre aspectos de seguridad y acceso a las áreas de intervención y en situaciones de crisis;
- Dar asesoría continua al Jefe de Misión y Coordinadores de Base en la toma de decisiones sobre movilidad, reubicaciones temporales o retorno progresivo del personal expatriado;
- Actuar como punto focal en foros, grupos de trabajo y espacios de coordinación cuando requerido.

**Modalidad de Trabajo**

- Tiempo parcial: El consultor trabajará a tiempo parcial, con una dedicación estimada de 20 horas semanales;
- Reunión semanal en oficina: El consultor deberá asistir a la oficina de la misión en Caracas o en la ubicación correspondiente almenos una vez por semana para reuniones puntuales con el jefe de misión y los gerentes de las bases;
- El consultor trabajará de forma hibirida (presencial y remota) para el desarrollo de documentos y la ejecución de actividades.
- Supervisión y Reporte: el Consultor de Seguridad reportará directamente al Jefe de Misión y colaborará estrechamente con los gerentes de las diferentes bases de COOPI en Venezuela.

**Productos Esperados**

- Reportes periodicos de contexto, riesgos relevantes y recomendaciones operativas;
- Documentos de seguridad de misión y bases revisados y actualizados según prioridades acordadas;
- Registro actualizado de incidentes y recomendaciones de seguimiento, cuando aplique;
- Sesiones periódicas de briefing, inducción o actualización de seguridad para personal de COOPI, según necesidades.
- Recomendaciones puntuales sobre movimientos, misiones a terreno, reubicaciones temporales, hibernación, evacuación o retorno progresivo, cuando el contexto lo requiera;
- Propuestas de mejora continua en la seguridad de la misión.

**Perfil deseado**

**ESENCIALES**

- Licenciatura en áreas relacionadas con Seguridad, Gestión de Riesgos, Ciencias Sociales, Derecho Internacional, o áreas afines. Preferible formación en Seguridad Humanitaria;
- Experiencia profesional:
- Mínimo de 5 años de experiencia en el ámbito de la seguridad en misiones humanitarias o en contextos de alto riesgo.
- Experiencia en desarrollo e implementación de planes de seguridad, protocolos de evacuación y manejo de crisis.
- Experiencia en el diseño y ejecución de entrenamientos en seguridad.
- Conocimiento del contexto de seguridad en Venezuela y en regiones de riesgo para organizaciones humanitarias.
- Capacitación en primeros auxilios, seguridad personal y manejo de crisis.
- Capacidad para elaborar informes detallados y claros.
- Habilidad para trabajar en equipo, tomar decisiones rápidas y liderar situaciones de emergencia.
- Capacidad de trabajar de forma autónoma (organización, planificación del trabajo, desarrollo analítico, síntesis) y en contextos con elevadas complejidades y bajo presión (cumplir con fechas limitadas);
- Motivación, honestidad, actitud pro-activa y resolutiva, sentido común, discreción;
- Respeto de la visión y misión de COOPI y de su carta de valores;
- Dominio del español (oral y escrito)
- Manejo de herramientas informáticas (sistema operativo Microsoft Windows) y ofimáticas (Microsoft Office: Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook);
- Nacionalidad Venezolana o permiso de residencia en el país;

**DESEABLES**

- Experiencia previa en Cooperación Internacional, Acción Humanitaria y/o afines;
- Idiomas: inglés altamente valorado;
- Disponibilidad inmediata o a la brevedad.

## **The full Request for Proposals (RFP) document is available at the following link:** [**https://drive.google.com/drive/u/1/folders/1uov69XvQzz7njxqHmtIdwTmaIZF2ekdB**](https://drive.google.com/drive/u/1/folders/1uov69XvQzz7njxqHmtIdwTmaIZF2ekdB)

## **The Organisation**

CEFA (the European Committee for Training and Agriculture) is an Italian non-governmental organisation established in 1972 and active in 10 countries in Africa, Latin America and Eastern Europe. CEFA’s medium- and long-term interventions in the agricultural, environmental and human rights sectors are focused on the sustainable development of communities, achieved by involving local counterparts and by promoting the active participation of the beneficiaries.

## **Project Summary**

The GROW project – Gender-Responsive Opportunities for Water Access, Livelihood Recovery and Climate Resilience for Vulnerable Communities in West Pokot County (AID 012999/01/2) – is an 18-month initiative funded by the Italian Agency for Development Cooperation (AICS) and implemented by CEFA, with PERUR – Rays of Hope as local partner, in West Pokot County, in the wards of Kodich, Kapchok and Kiwawa.

The project responds to humanitarian needs and promotes the resilience of vulnerable communities affected by recurrent climate shocks in arid and semi-arid areas, with particular attention to women, girls, children and persons with disabilities. Its integrated approach combines water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), agriculture and food security, livelihoods, gender equality, natural-resource management and disaster risk reduction.

In particular, to contribute to women’s economic empowerment and livelihood diversification, the project will:

- establish or strengthen nine women’s groups, comprising 20 members each and 180 women in total, across Kodich, Kapchok and Kiwawa Wards;
- deliver a five-day Training of Trainers course covering financial literacy, household financial management, Group Savings and Loans methodology and business planning for 27 representatives of the women’s groups;
- support the delivery of two-day cascade-training sessions to the nine groups, reaching all 180 women;
- support the groups in developing, reviewing and finalising viable business plans and income-generating activity proposals;
- provide eligible groups with start-up grants following CEFA’s approval of their business plans and grant-readiness documentation;
- provide technical assistance, on-the-job coaching and monitoring during the establishment and initial operation of the supported income-generating activities.

## **Tasks and Responsibilities of the Consultant**

Under the supervision of the Project Manager and in coordination with the Field Coordinator, the CEFA Gender/IGA Field Officer, the monitoring, evaluation, accountability and learning (MEAL) function and other relevant project staff, the consultant will provide specialised support for the implementation and monitoring of Activity A.2.3. The assignment shall cover group assessment, development and delivery of training, review of business plans, preparation of grant-readiness recommendations, post-training and post-disbursement coaching, monitoring of Group Savings and Loans operations and evaluation of supported income-generating activities.

The consultant will coordinate closely with CEFA staff, the relevant West Pokot County technical departments and community structures in the target wards.

The consultant shall comply with AICS requirements and CEFA internal policies and procedures, including safeguarding, prevention of sexual exploitation, abuse and harassment, data protection, accountability to affected populations, safety and security, and conflict-sensitive implementation.

**Inception, Group Assessment and Planning**

The consultant shall:

- assess the organisational, financial and business capacity of each of the nine women’s groups;
- support confirmation of group membership and selection of three Training of Trainers representatives per group;
- establish an initial group and IGA baseline;
- prepare an inception report, detailed workplan, group-support schedule and monitoring framework.

**Development and Delivery of Training**

The consultant shall:

- prepare or adapt practical, gender-responsive and low-literacy-friendly training materials on financial literacy, GSL management, record keeping, group governance, business planning and basic business management;
- deliver a five-day Training of Trainers course to 27 representatives;
- assess participants’ learning through appropriate pre- and post-training tools;
- support and, where necessary, co-facilitate the two-day cascade training for each of the nine groups; and
- ensure that training promotes women’s meaningful participation and addresses possible household or community tensions.

**Business-Plan Review and Grant Readiness**

For each of the nine groups, the consultant shall:

- assess the proposed IGA’s market, organisational, technical and financial feasibility;
- review projected costs, revenues, cash flow, risks and required permits or approvals;
- verify that the proposed expenditure is realistic, eligible and consistent with the available grant;
- support finalisation of the business plan, budget, implementation schedule and performance milestones;
- assess the group’s governance, financial controls and grant-management capacity; and
- submit a documented recommendation on readiness for financing.

The consultant’s recommendation shall be advisory. Final approval of business plans and grant disbursements shall remain the exclusive responsibility of CEFA.

**Follow-Up and Technical Assistance to Supported IGAs**

Following disbursement of the first grant instalment, the consultant shall provide structured coaching and monitoring to each group throughout the four-month period preceding assessment for the second instalment. The consultant shall:

- verify use of grant resources against the approved business plan, budget and grant agreement;
- review financial, procurement, stock and sales records;
- coach groups on business management, pricing, marketing, record keeping and working-capital management;
- monitor GSL and IGA performance and progress against agreed milestones;
- identify challenges and agree corrective actions with clear deadlines;
- facilitate relevant market and institutional linkages; and
- document progress, lessons learned and issues requiring referral to CEFA.

At the end of the four-month period, the consultant shall assess each group’s use of the first instalment, business progress, GSL performance, governance and readiness for the second instalment, and submit a technical recommendation to CEFA.

**Monitoring and Evaluation of GSL Groups and IGAs**

The consultant shall develop and apply practical monitoring tools covering:

- GSL membership, attendance, savings, loans, repayments, arrears, social-fund management, records and group governance;
- IGA establishment, use of grant resources, sales, costs, cash flow, profitability, stock and assets, market access, achievement of business-plan milestones and sustainability; and
- corrective actions, outstanding risks and member participation.

The consultant shall use CEFA-approved attendance, monitoring and reporting templates and provide timely information to CEFA’s MEAL function, disaggregated where applicable by group, ward, age and disability, and contribute to measuring the percentage of trained women who initiate an IGA.

**Coordination and Synergies**

The consultant shall:

- coordinate regularly with the Project Manager, Field Coordinator, Gender/IGA Field Officer, MEAL staff and CEFA administrative personnel;
- liaise with relevant West Pokot County departments, including officers responsible for gender, cooperatives, trade, enterprise development, agriculture and livestock, depending on the selected IGAs;
- identify suitable market actors, suppliers, buyers, financial institutions and business-development services;
- promote practical synergies that add demonstrable value and do not create conflicts of interest; and
- participate in coordination, review and validation meetings requested by CEFA.

## **Duration and Timeline of the Consultancy**

The consultancy will commence upon contract signature and will be implemented on a part-time, deliverable-based basis during the GROW project period, ending no later than 30 September 2027. The maximum level of effort is 90 working days. The consultancy shall cover the full implementation cycle from the initial group assessment and training through to the completion of the four-month period between the first grant instalment and the assessment for the second instalment. The exact dates of the four-month monitoring period will depend on the actual date on which the first instalment is disbursed to each group. Where first-instalment disbursement dates differ among groups, the consultant shall adapt the workplan accordingly and maintain a separate monitoring schedule for each group.

The consultancy days shall be distributed on a non-consecutive basis throughout the assignment. The workplan shall provide for regular and meaningful contact with each group during the four-month monitoring period, rather than concentrating all consultancy days at the beginning or end of the assignment.

## **Minimum Requirements**

This procurement procedure is open exclusively to individual consultants. To be eligible for evaluation, the applicant must meet and provide evidence of all the following minimum requirements:

1. Hold at least a bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Economics, Finance, Accounting, Entrepreneurship, Agribusiness, Cooperative Management, Development Studies or another field directly relevant to the assignment;
2. Hold a valid Tax Compliance Certificate at the application deadline;
3. Be legally authorised to undertake paid consultancy services in Kenya for the entire duration of the assignment;
4. Be available to personally perform the assignment, including regular field visits to Kodich, Kapchok and Kiwawa Wards in West Pokot County, in accordance with the approved workplan and implementation schedule; and
5. Have the professional and technical capacity required to carry out the tasks and deliverables described in these Terms of Reference.

**ABOUT Catalyste+ (formerly CESO)**

Catalyste+, formerly known as Canadian Executive Service Organization (CESO), was established as a Canadian not-for-profit organization in 1967, with a vision of creating stronger economies for better lives. We empower people, businesses, communities and institutions to drive economic development and improve lives around the world by sharing the expertise and experiences of our volunteers. Catalyste+ has been active in Southeast Asia for more than 50 years—notably in the Philippines, Vietnam, Myanmar, Cambodia, and Lao PDR, with plans to continue expansion in the region.

Reporting to the Country Representative, the Project Officer will contribute towards fulfilling Catalyste+’s mission of strengthening economic and social well-being in Lao PDR. The Project Officer will support the Country Representative’s role, and will develop and maintain effective relationships with partners, stakeholders, Catalyste+ Advisors and Catalyste+ staff. He/she is responsible for providing overall programmatic and logistical support to design and implement activities in selected sectors, with the aim to advance Catalyste+’s gender equality and women’s economic empowerment (GE/WEE) objectives, in accordance with approved Catalyste+ policies and guidelines.

**PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES:**

**PROGRAM SUPPORT**

- Participate in stakeholder consultations to support the development of GE/WEE and sector strategies and the integration of local knowledge, in alignment with donor priorities;
- Support partnership development, assignment design and applications with the aim to meet/surpass country targets and objectives;
- Support timely recruitment and deployment of appropriately skilled Catalyste+ Advisors by collaborating with the Volunteer Services team and delivering all logistical support as requested;
- Support Catalyste+ Advisors in carrying out work plans, ensuring they are in line with GE/WEE objectives, local context and Catalyste+ guidelines/policies;
- Support the collection of content and the development of communications and marketing materials, as requested by the Communications and Engagement team, with the aim to showcase the impact of Catalyste+’s work to key stakeholders;
- Represent Catalyste+ as a brand ambassador at relevant meetings/events in lieu of the Country Representative, when needed;
- Contribute to annual work plans and other donor reports.

**OPERATIONS SUPPORT**

- Collaborate with the Volunteer Services team to ensure efficient processes are in place to manage the arrival, placement and departure of Catalyste+ Advisors to/from Lao PDR, including the development of tools (e.g. online surveys, customer service questionnaires);
- Assist with arranging the logistics of in-country travel for Catalyste+ Advisors, as needed;
- Assist the Country Representative in arranging, in collaboration with partners, the accommodation, food, transport and other services to be provided to the Catalyste+ Advisors, including the identification and approval of comfortable accommodations in a secure and safe location;
- Arrange meetings between Catalyste+ Advisors and partners related to Catalyste+ assignments;
- Provide translation/interpretation to support partnership and assignment development and implementation;
- Assist the Country Representative in providing orientation to Catalyste+ Advisors and monitoring the progress of Catalyste+ Advisors’ work (e.g. participating in entrance and exit meetings between Catalyste+ Advisors and partners in lieu of the Country Representative, as needed);
- File, manage and organize documents related to the Lao PDR;
- Adhere to Catalyste+’s policies and processes related to security, emergency preparedness, code of conduct, conflict of interest, misconduct, harassment and other administrative matters;
- Contribute to Catalyste+’s efforts to standardize organizational processes and customer experiences;
- Assist the Country Representative with accounting, budget management and preparation of financial reports to the Senior Program Manager for Asia;
- Provide any other duties to support Catalyste+’s work in Lao PDR, as required.

**ORGANIZATIONAL LEARNING**

- Support gender-based analysis in project design, implementation and learning processes;
- Actively collect quality data from partners, clients and other stakeholders and share it in a timely manner as requested by the Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning team;
- Develop strategic, evidence-based suggestions to improve long term results based on learning from implemented activities, partners, volunteers and other stakeholders.

**TO BE SUCCESSFUL IN THIS ROLE, YOU WILL REQUIRE:**

**MINIMUM LEVEL OF EDUCATION**

Bachelor’s degree in business, economics, international development, or another related field is desirable.

**WORK EXPERIENCE AND SKILLS**

- Willing to work in a development organization with international colleagues;
- Deep understanding of international development issues, notably GE/WEE, the environment and climate change, private sector development, value chains and governance; hands-on experience with these issues preferred.
- Collaborative team spirit, good attitude and excellent customer service;
- Strong communication and interpersonal skills;
- Fluency in English, with an excellent command and working proficiency of both the spoken and written language, including the ability to prepare reports and other documents;
- Good computer proficiency and skilled at using word processors, spreadsheets, etc.;
- Cultural sensitivity and ability to deal with sensitive issues in a diplomatic fashion.
- Ready to learn, adapt and grow within the Catalyste+ work environment in Lao PDR;
- Extensive experience (two or more years) of working with volunteers and volunteer organizations would be an asset.

**Application deadline: Wednesday, November 11, 2026**

Catalyste+ is an equal-opportunity employer and encourages applications from all qualified candidates. Accommodations are available upon request for candidates in all aspects of the selection process.

## Mission description

Under the hierarchical supervision of the Deputy Head of the Education, Higher Education and Research Unit France (Paris), with a close functional link to the Project Secretariat (Rome). The Coordinator of the Expertise France component – MED CAMPUSES – corresponds to the focal point of Expertise France within the Project Secretariat and vis-à-vis the Steering Committee.

Based mainly at the Expertise France office in Rabat, with regular missions to countries of intervention (Tunisia, Morocco, Jordan, Egypt, Lebanon, and Libya) and participation in project governance bodies (Rome/Brussels).

The Coordinator oversees the entire MED CAMPUS component, the centerpiece of the project South anchorage. They represent Expertise France within a tripartite consortium comprising MAECI-Italy FIAP-Spain, and EF-France, operating under the “Team Europe” framework. The ability to work effectively within a consortium and maintain strong institutional relationships—with the European Commission as donor; Italian partners (MAECI/EUTALIA/Uni-Italia); Spanish partners (FIAP); and key stakeholders, including the Secretariat of the Union for the Mediterranean (UfM) and UNIMED—is critical to the project’s success.

The position requires a **confirmed project management profile**, with proven expertise in s**etting up and monitoring grants** (sub-granting mechanisms towards MED Campus, leveraged grants at Téthys), in **multi-budget management country**, and a real **diplomatic and partnership capacity** to navigate between donor, implementing partners and higher education institutions. Knowledge of the academic sector is an asset but not the heart of the position: it is above all an operational, financial, and institutional steering post.

**Key responsibilities**

*Strategic management and governance*

- Oversee and manage the entire programmatic cycle of the 8 MED Campus (selection, needs assessment, development of action plans, deployment of technical assistance and grants);
- Ensure programmatic consistency across the 8 MED Campuses and alignment with the strategic directions approved by the Steering Committee and the Secretariat;
- Oversee the operationalisation of the Intra-MENA Mobility Scheme within the Expertise France component;
- Lead, in collaboration with Téthys, the programmatic and financial dimension of the crosscutting gender equality and inclusion project.

*Team management and coordination*

- Supervise the Grant Officer and ensure the functional coordination of the Higher Education expert and experts mobilised in the short term on the component;
- Work in close coordination with the Project Support Unit (PSU) and with the headquarters in Paris.

*Institutional and partnership relations*

- Represent Expertise France within the Secretariat and before the Steering Committee; maintain relations with the Directorate-General for the Middle East, North Africa and the Gulf (DG MENA) of the European Commission (donor);
- Ensure institutional coordination with the Italian unit (MAECI) and FIAP on the Spanish side, and develop partnership relations in a "Team Europe" consortium approach;
- To be the privileged interlocutor of Expertise France with the French institutions involved in the project — the Téthys network (Aix-Marseille University), Campus France, and the MESR — and ensure that their respective contributions are properly coordinated with the implementation of the MED Campus;
- Maintain regular dialogue with UfM, UNIMED and other academic partner networks.

*Administrative, financial and grant management*

- Manage and arbitrate the EF component budget, oversee administrative, financial and procurement procedures (in liaison with the USP team in Rabat);
- Supervise the grant mechanism at MED CAMPUS and the grant to Téthys (setup, contracting, financial monitoring, compliance) in conjunction with the Grant Officer;

*Reporting and accountability*

- Ensure all the narrative and financial reporting for the component, in coordination with the Secretariat and the other Team Europe units, including the preparation of the inception report and the semi-annual reports;
- Ensure internal reporting at Expertise France (progress reports, alerts, monitoring indicators) in collaboration with headquarters.

## Project or context description

The **Mediterranean University** project is a Euro-Mediterranean initiative jointly led by three entities: the Italian Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MAECI) as lead consortium, FIAP for Spain, Expertise France for France, in indirect management of the European Commission. The project is **36 months, with a budget of €12.2 M in total (including €2.6 M for EF including €100,000 in co-financing from the MEAE),** covering all countries in the Mediterranean basin, including **10 Southern Neighbourhood partner countries.**

This initiative aims to sustainably strengthen cooperation in the fields of higher education, research and innovation between the European Union and partner countries on the southern shore of the Mediterranean. It is part of the Pact for the Mediterranean, which makes education and youth priorities for rapprochement between the two shores.

The action is based on the establishment of the Secretariat of the "Mediterranean University", in charge of coordinating two complementary pillars:

- **University Consortiums:** already mature alliances (North-South) quickly delivering joint degrees, mobility, and mutual recognition—piloted on the Italian.
- **The MED Campus:** hosted by universities on the southern shore, organized around 8 thematic priorities linked to the Pact for the Mediterranean, aiming to build these universities' institutional capacity so that they eventually become consortium leaders ("seeds" of the next generation of consortia).

**Expertise France’s** role is specifically concerned with the overall coordination of the MED Campus component as described below.

- Operational and administrative management of support provided to the 8 thematic MED Campus, methodological consistency between them and with the program’s strategic directions;
- Grants linked to the action plans of each campus;
- Support for the operationalization of an Intra-Mena Mobility Scheme;
- Technical assistance on request to selected institutions;
- Strategic articulation with key partners: **Tethys** (university network coordinated by Aix-Marseille Université, 100+ universities/22 countries), the **MESR, among others;**
- Steering, with Téthys, the cross-cutting gender equality and inclusion project

This is a first phase that will notably allow for the establishment of common governance, launch two university consortia and up to eight MED Campuses, and lay the foundations for a larger-scale deployment of the initiative. A second phase could be expected after these three years.

## Required profile

- Higher education (Bac+5) in international project management, international relations, political science or equivalent;
- Proven experience (8-10 years) in managing EU-funded international cooperation projects, ideally in indirect management;
- Solid expertise in grant management and sub-granting mechanisms, multi-country budget management;
- Excellent capacity for negotiation and management of multi-stakeholder partnership relations (donors, ministries, counterpart agencies);
- Experience working in an international consortium and a multicultural environment;
- Knowledge of the higher education sector is desired but not essential;
- Current imperative English (working language of the project); French and/or Arabic are assets;
- Availability for regular trips in the region.

